  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 315 AM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nPlentiful monsoon moisture is expected to remain in place over the \narea for at least Tuesday and Wednesday as temperatures moderate a \nlittle closer to seasonal averages. We will still be warmer than \naverage, but perhaps by just 3 to 5 degrees compared to 10 to 12 \ndegrees. Ensemble mean precipitable water remains at or above the \nclimatological 90th percentile during this period, suggesting daily \nchances for at least scattered if not numerous to widespread showers \nand thunderstorms. Currently, ensembles are most optimistic \nabout rainfall chances on Tuesday for the majority of our area. \nIt's too early to get into specifics concerning storm mode and \nsevere potential at this time, but the environment appears to \nsupport a few isolated strong to severe thunderstorms each day. \n\nA pattern change is expected to occur across the western CONUS on \nThursday. A shortwave trough riding over the top of the ridge will \npush across the area. Models are in fairly good agreement showing \nupper level winds turning more westerly across much of the I-80 \ncorridor and Great Basin region, advecting much drier air into the \narea and suppressing monsoon moisture to the south. Meanwhile, the \nupper level ridge will try to retrograde back to the west and \nbroaden itself significantly further to the west. Therefore, we\nwill probably completely lose our tap to the monsoon by the end\nof the work week. Recycled moisture and a few upper level \nshortwaves moving over the top of the ridge may still keep \nisolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms in the \nforecast, but the outlook is not very optimistic for widespread \nrainfall. As the ridge sets up over the southwestern US and \namplifies again, we can expect another warming trend to \ngradually nudge temperatures upward into next weekend.",
